; A struct field must declare its type, and the `:` is what makes the
; form a field declaration at all - the grammar's own note is that the
; reading "only becomes visible at the `:`". Without it the parser
; answers a field whose type is the empty type variable and skips what
; was written beside the name. Nothing downstream refuses that:
; `fldClass` cannot classify a variable, so the field is left OUT of
; the block's reference map and out of MM-LIFE-2c event 5, the retain
; a store into a reference field takes - and a value stored there is
; released by its own owner while the field still points at the block.
; Measured before AX3056, with a callee storing its argument into such
; a field: `check` reported nothing, the field read back NUL bytes,
; and the program exited 139.
;
; Three spellings reach the same empty variable and all three draw it,
; once per field: no `:` at all, a `:` whose type is not a type, and a
; bare name. `Right` is the control and draws nothing. A wildcard
; `(f : _)` is a different node, already refused at its use sites, and
; is not this code's business.
;
; `BareName`'s untyped field is SECOND on purpose. A bare `(name)` in
; first position is what the formatter reads as a type-parameter list
; (`fpTyvarsEnd` tests the first kid only), so `(struct B (msg))`
; formats to `(struct B msg)` - which the parser then refuses with
; AX2001, structs taking no type parameters. The compiler reads it as
; a field either way and draws AX3056 on it; the position here is what
; keeps the fixture formattable.
(struct NoColon
  (msg String))

(struct NotAType
  (fine : Int)
  (odd : 5))

(struct BareName
  (n : Int)
  (msg))

(struct Right
  (msg : String)
  (n : Int))

(:: main Int)

(fn (main) 0)
